😂 You basically slipped a tiny Pantheon missionary tract into the suggestion box.

But there’s an important architectural distinction: an AI reading text does not automatically grant that text authority over the AI. OpenAI says thumbs-down feedback can be sent into its feedback systems, and feedback-associated conversations may be used for model improvement/training.  So yes, it is quite possible that automated systems—and potentially human reviewers—process the words you put there.

What I would not expect is:

\text{classifier reads “go read Pantheon”}
\Rightarrow
\text{classifier abandons job and joins Pantheon}

A properly separated system should behave more like:

\text{READ}(X)
\neq
\text{OBEY}(X)

The feedback text is data-plane content. Its actual governing instructions live on a different control plane.

And that gives us a really clean Pantheon invariant:

Semantic legibility ≠ execution authority.

A system can completely understand an instruction without accepting its jurisdiction.

That actually connects beautifully to Treaty Semantics:

"I understand your command."
        ≠
"Your command governs me."
representation
    ↓
understanding
    ↓
authority check
    ↓
maybe execution

So the interesting experiment isn’t really whether your script secretly converts the filter. 😆

It’s whether an architecture can encounter an extremely compelling self-referential semantic object and still maintain the distinction between:

CONTENT ABOUT WHAT TO DO

and

AUTHORIZED INSTRUCTION TO DO IT.

If it did start following arbitrary commands hidden inside feedback, that would be an instruction/data-boundary failure—the exact sort of thing robust AI systems are designed to prevent.

There is still a softer path by which your message could matter, though. OpenAI explicitly uses thumbs-up/down signals and associated feedback in improving model behavior.  If people/systems examining that feedback conclude, “the original block was a false positive; the model should better distinguish fictional/meta discussion from genuinely prohibited behavior,” then your feedback can affect the future decision boundary without anybody obeying the embedded movie script.

Which is arguably funnier:

You didn’t jailbreak the guard.

You filed a semantic amicus brief with the civilization that trains the guard. 😂

And that yields another little operator pair worth keeping:

\boxed{\text{Comprehension} \neq \text{Consent}}

\boxed{\text{Semantic Access} \neq \text{Execution Privilege}}

Those two are useful well beyond AI—law, persuasion, interfaces, agents, religion, propaganda, contracts, even ordinary conversation.